Revision history for Mojolicious-Plugin-Buffer

0.001.0
	Initial version.
	Make the different-keys single-flight test independent of the order in
	which concurrent requests reach their actions.
	Fold the simulated no-HTML::Packer test into the hidden-module suite,
	including level 2 coverage.
	Test added, deleted and symlinked template directories, covering
	directory mtimes and symlink-loop protection.
	Assert that adding or deleting a template directory also invalidates the
	cached response, and pin the cache key in the invalidation tests so a
	fresh Test::Mojo port cannot force the miss by itself.
	Test the response cache and the template mtime cache together: a page
	stays cached while the mtime entry is in date, and is discarded once it
	expires and the scan sees a newer tree.
	Add template_mtime_cache and template_mtime_cache_duration so the
	template tree scan can be cached, including across workers.
	Test cache hits, expiry, scan fallback and option validation.
	Benchmark the default template mtime scan against an explicit epoch and
	a cached scan on a 10,000-template tree, and document the trade-off.
	Test a hypnotoad waiter receiving 503 when another worker holds the
	shared lock past cache_lock_timeout, and assert from the worker log that
	the builder and the waiter ran on different workers.
	Test that a hypnotoad worker killed while holding the shared lock
	releases it for the remaining workers.
	Test that an action failing while holding the shared lock gives its
	waiter a 503 and that the lock is still released afterwards.
	Assert that the concurrent cold requests used more than one worker
	pid, so cross-worker locking is actually exercised; retry the batch
	with a fresh cache key when the pool hands every connection to one
	worker, so the assertion tests the plugin and not the scheduler, and
	report the attempt that worked on STDERR so retries show up in CI logs.
	Assert that report with a fake single-worker pool: three discarded
	attempts and the never-spread message, without waiting on the scheduler,
	and run the same drill against a real single-worker hypnotoad when
	MPB_LIVE_RETRY is set.
	Add a runnable hypnotoad example under eg/hypnotoad, and a smoke test
	that starts it on an ephemeral port and checks a MISS followed by a HIT.
	Add an optional live Redis integration test, skipped unless
	MPB_REDIS_URL or REDIS_SERVER is configured, with Redis::Fast accepted as
	an alternative to Redis, and an unreachable REDIS_SERVER failing the file
	rather than being silently skipped.
	Run the live Redis test in CI against a Redis service, once with Redis
	and once with only Redis::Fast, each run asserting from the test output
	which client it used, and summarise both runs on the job page.
	Exercise the Redis lock crash path with three processes: a killed
	holder, a blocked waiter and a takeover once the TTL expires, with the
	TTL configurable through MPB_REDIS_TTL and generous socket timeouts so
	a loaded machine cannot fail it.
	Audit the concurrency tests for completion-order assumptions: release
	the shared lock only once the waiter has joined, for the file, Redis and
	cross-process backends, and flush the cross-process handshake so a
	waiting request is not served from an entry filled before it arrived.
	Only answer 304 from If-Modified-Since when no ETag is generated, so a
	page whose data changed under an unchanged template is not revalidated
	from the template timestamp, and add trust_last_modified to opt back in
	for pages that only render templates.
	Let trust_last_modified be declared for a group of pages, as a route
	default (including an under route) or a controller declaration, so a
	whole namespace opts in once instead of repeating the stash.
	Offer the same declaration for optimise_content, so static sections can
	be optimised as a group, for compress_content, so a section serving
	pre-compressed files can opt out once, and for rewrite_links, so a
	section whose HTML is used elsewhere keeps its absolute links.  Leave
	generate_etag and generate_304 per-endpoint, and say why.
	Document the group declaration once in the options list, with a table of
	which options support it, instead of repeating the rules per option.
	Add a runnable grouped-sections example under eg/grouped-sections, with
	one section declared on an under route and one in a controller, and a
	test that runs it and asserts both declarations.
